{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":302008,"name":"Bartosz Sławecki","username":"bswck","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/bswck/{size}/34363_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-18T17:46:00.826Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI am pleased to present PEP 846, a well-received idea to recognize docstrings of type aliases in Python.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat I like the most about it is that it looks like an easy win for the language.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt’s focused, it has support in most relevant tools, and it’s a well-established practice among many Python programmers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-302008-abstract-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-302008-abstract-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eAbstract\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis PEP proposes preserving a string literal immediately following a \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#type\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003etype\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e statement as the resulting type alias object’s \u003ccode\u003e__doc__\u003c/code\u003e attribute, exposing that documentation through \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#module-ast\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003east\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e, and displaying it through \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/pydoc.html#module-pydoc\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003epydoc\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#help\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003ehelp()\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e. It follows the placement already supported by source-based documentation tools. The parser stores the docstring in a new optional \u003ccode\u003edoc\u003c/code\u003e field on \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.TypeAlias\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003east.TypeAlias\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e instead of creating a separate \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.Expr\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003east.Expr\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e node for it. \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.get_docstring\" title=\"(in Python v3.14)\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003east.get_docstring()\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e retrieves alias docstrings from this field.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-302008-full-pep-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-302008-full-pep-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFull PEP\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0846/\"\u003e\n  \u003cheader class=\"source\"\u003e\n      \u003cimg src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/7/8/7808f673c94c5abc6cdd738548e39c7ae50dc418.png\" class=\"site-icon\" alt=\"\" data-dominant-color=\"979F6B\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\"\u003e\n\n      \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0846/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePython Enhancement Proposals (PEPs)\u003c/a\u003e\n  \u003c/header\u003e\n\n  \u003carticle class=\"onebox-body\"\u003e\n    \u003cimg width=\"200\" height=\"200\" src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/8/e/8eb386d43fc93f43e4e34ee56dd1c3ebf682e66d.png\" class=\"thumbnail onebox-avatar\" alt=\"\" data-dominant-color=\"9EAC81\"\u003e\n\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0846/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePEP 846 – Docstrings for Type Aliases | peps.python.org\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003eThis PEP proposes preserving a string literal immediately following a type statement as the resulting type alias object’s __doc__ attribute, exposing that documentation through ast, and displaying it through pydoc and help(). It follows the placement...\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\n  \u003c/article\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"onebox-metadata\"\u003e\n    \n    \n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv style=\"clear: both\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\n\u003ch4\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-302008-previous-idea-discussion-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-302008-previous-idea-discussion-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003ePrevious idea discussion\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/docstrings-for-type-aliases\"\u003ehttps://discuss.python.org/t/docstrings-for-type-aliases\u003c/a\u003e (\u003cstrong\u003eplease see this for mentions of a more general “variable docstrings” problem and why this PEP does not address it!\u003c/strong\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch4\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-302008-play-with-this-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-302008-play-with-this-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003ePlay with this\u003c/h4\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"onebox githubfolder\" data-onebox-src=\"https://github.com/johnslavik/cpython/tree/gh-156925\"\u003e\n  \u003cheader class=\"source\"\u003e\n      \u003cimg src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/b/a/bad3e5f9ad67c1ddf145107ce7032ac1d7b22563.svg\" class=\"site-icon\" alt=\"\" data-dominant-color=\"\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"\u003e\n\n      \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/johnslavik/cpython/tree/gh-156925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003egithub.com\u003c/a\u003e\n  \u003c/header\u003e\n\n  \u003carticle class=\"onebox-body\"\u003e\n    \u003ch3\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/johnslavik/cpython/tree/gh-156925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eGitHub - johnslavik/cpython at gh-156925\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/johnslavik/cpython/tree/gh-156925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003egh-156925\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class=\"label1\"\u003eThe Python programming language. Contribute to johnslavik/cpython development by creating an account on GitHub.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n  \u003c/article\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"onebox-metadata\"\u003e\n    \n    \n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv style=\"clear: both\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRun with \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/savannahostrowski/every-python\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003eevery-python\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"bash\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-bash\"\u003euvx every-python run --repo johnslavik/cpython gh-156925 -- python\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-18T17:50:20.081Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":75,"reads":179,"readers_count":178,"score":745.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Bartosz Sławecki","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0846/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 846 – Docstrings for Type Aliases | peps.python.org","clicks":81},{"url":"https://github.com/savannahostrowski/every-python","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - savannahostrowski/every-python: A utility to build any commit of CPython, even with the JIT enabled · GitHub","clicks":9},{"url":"https://github.com/johnslavik/cpython/tree/gh-156925","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - johnslavik/cpython at gh-156925 · GitHub","clicks":3},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#help","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Built-in Functions — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":1},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.get_docstring","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"ast — Abstract syntax trees — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":1},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#type","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"7. Simple statements — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":1},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#module-ast","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"ast — Abstract syntax trees — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":1},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.TypeAlias","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"ast — Abstract syntax trees — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/docstrings-for-type-aliases/108901","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"Docstrings for type aliases","clicks":0},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.Expr","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"ast — Abstract syntax trees — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":0},{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/pydoc.html#module-pydoc","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"pydoc — Documentation generator and online help system — Python 3.14.7 documentation","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/docstrings-for-type-aliases/108901/25","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"Docstrings for type aliases","clicks":1},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/class-like-syntax-for-union-type-aliases-and-natural-docstrings/109103/7","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"Class-like syntax for union type aliases and natural docstrings","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/syntactic-sugar-for-union-cases-in-match-statements/49274/28","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"Syntactic sugar for union cases in match statements","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":23}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":10313,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":23}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":23,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":302020,"name":"Dutcho","username":"Dutcho","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/dutcho/{size}/7074_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-18T19:48:24.035Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eNice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI see you chose to put \u003cem\u003edoctest\u003c/em\u003e support out of scope.\u003cbr\u003e\nPlease document in \u003cem\u003erejected ideas\u003c/em\u003e including motivation. And perhaps in \u003cem\u003ehow to teach\u003c/em\u003e, that this is an exception to the rule that \u003cem\u003edocstring\u003c/em\u003es can have \u003cem\u003edoctest\u003c/em\u003es.\u003cbr\u003e\n(Or -my preference- make it in scope \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/smiling_face.png?v=15\" title=\":smiling_face:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":smiling_face:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-18T19:48:24.035Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":149,"readers_count":148,"score":54.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Dutcho","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":7138,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/2","reactions":[{"id":"+1","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302025,"name":"Maciej J. Mikulski","username":"mjmikulski","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mjmikulski/{size}/37517_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-18T21:15:17.939Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eDocstrings for type aliases are useful and worth supporting \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/flexed_biceps.png?v=15\" title=\":flexed_biceps:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":flexed_biceps:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePEP 846 reminded me of an old idea: class-like syntax for union type aliases. With that syntax, instead of attaching the following string to a \u003ccode\u003etype\u003c/code\u003e statement, a type alias could optionally have a class-like block with the familiar docstring placement:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003etype Timeout:\n    \"\"\"Maximum wait in seconds.\"\"\"\n    float\n    None\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ewith multiple expressions in the block forming a union.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI posted the idea separately here to avoid derailing this PEP discussion:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"quote quote-modified\" data-post=\"1\" data-topic=\"109103\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mjmikulski/48/37517_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"quote-title__text-content\"\u003e\n      \u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/class-like-syntax-for-union-type-aliases-and-natural-docstrings/109103\"\u003eClass-like syntax for union type aliases and natural docstrings\u003c/a\u003e \u003ca class=\"badge-category__wrapper \" href=\"/c/ideas/6\"\u003e\u003cspan data-category-id=\"6\" style=\"--category-badge-color: #3AB54A; --category-badge-text-color: #FFFFFF;\" data-drop-close=\"true\" class=\"badge-category --style-square \"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"badge-category__name\"\u003eIdeas\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n  \u003c/div\u003e\n  \u003cblockquote\u003e\n    In many type aliases, a union is the outermost operation. \nThe recent PEP 846 (docstrings for type aliases) reminded me of an old idea of mine. That PEP proposes to attach a string statement to the preceding type statement, because the current type statement (unlike class) has no natural place for a docstring. I propose the following class-like syntax that takes the union of all its members. \nFor example, instead of: \ntype Timeout = int | float | None\n\"\"\"Maximum wait in seconds; None waits indef…\n  \u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-18T21:15:17.939Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":143,"readers_count":142,"score":38.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Maciej J. Mikulski","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/class-like-syntax-for-union-type-aliases-and-natural-docstrings/109103","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"Class-like syntax for union type aliases and natural docstrings","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":93426,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302031,"name":"Joren Hammudoglu","username":"jorenham","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jorenham/{size}/27806_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-18T22:50:14.738Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks, this seems very useful, and I’d love to be able to apply this to the \u003ccode\u003enumpy.typing\u003c/code\u003e type aliases \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/slight_smile.png?v=15\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e .\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen reading the “Rejected Ideas”  section I had a bit of a déjà vu, which I then realized was the rejected \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0727/\"\u003ePEP 727\u003c/a\u003e from a while back. It proposed a mechanism for documenting type annotations, and therefore also type aliases, using a new dedicated \u003ccode\u003etyping.Doc\u003c/code\u003e class as \u003ccode\u003etyping.Annotated\u003c/code\u003e metadata.\u003cbr\u003e\nAnd since PEP 846 already talks about \u003ccode\u003eAnnotated\u003c/code\u003e as a rejected idea, I thought it might also be a good idea to mention PEP 727.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-18T22:50:14.738Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":133,"readers_count":132,"score":46.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Joren Hammudoglu","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0727/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 727 – Documentation in Annotated Metadata | peps.python.org","clicks":12}],"read":true,"user_title":"Regular","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":17749,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/4","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302072,"name":"Timothée Mazzucotelli","username":"pawamoy","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/pawamoy/{size}/5811_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-19T09:08:45.697Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eCongrats \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/bswck\"\u003e@bswck\u003c/a\u003e!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTools that find alias docstrings by looking at the following statement, or that rely on the alias node’s end position, need adjusting when parsing with Python 3.16.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYep, confirmed:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003e# pkg.py\ntype T = int | None\n\"\"\"Hello.\"\"\"\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e(assuming Griffe is installed in respective envs)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"console\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-console\"\u003e% python -c 'import griffe; print(griffe.load(\"pkg\")[\"T\"].docstring)'\n\u0026lt;griffe._internal.models.Docstring object at 0x7f71caae4ad0\u0026gt;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"console\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-console\"\u003e% every-python run gh-156925 --repo johnslavik/cpython -- python -c 'import griffe; print(griffe.load(\"pkg\")[\"T\"].docstring)'\nNone\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAdded to the backlog \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/slight_smile.png?v=15\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-19T09:09:03.439Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":119,"readers_count":118,"score":58.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Timothée Mazzucotelli","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":6022,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302265,"name":"Bartosz Sławecki","username":"bswck","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/bswck/{size}/34363_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-22T13:48:39.776Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Dutcho\" data-post=\"2\" data-topic=\"109116\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/dutcho/48/7074_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Dutcho:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI see you chose to put \u003cem\u003edoctest\u003c/em\u003e support out of scope.\u003cbr\u003e\nPlease document in \u003cem\u003erejected ideas\u003c/em\u003e including motivation. And perhaps in \u003cem\u003ehow to teach\u003c/em\u003e, that this is an exception to the rule that \u003cem\u003edocstring\u003c/em\u003es can have \u003cem\u003edoctest\u003c/em\u003es.\u003cbr\u003e\n(Or -my preference- make it in scope \u003cimg src=\"https://emoji.discourse-cdn.com/apple/smiling_face.png?v=15\" title=\":smiling_face:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":smiling_face:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’ve included it in the scope. The update of the PEP should land soon.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-22T13:48:39.776Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":86,"readers_count":85,"score":37.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases","display_username":"Bartosz Sławecki","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":10313,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-846-docstrings-for-type-aliases/109116/6","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302302,"name":"Doug Hoskisson","username":"beauxq","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/beauxq/{size}/15042_2.png","created_at":"2026-09-22T21:14:54.841Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003e+0 on the change.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis PEP has no known security implications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are tools that automatically look for code in docstrings and run it as tests.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m imagining some freak accident where someone puts some code that should never be run in a string in a place where they don’t expect it to be run because they weren’t aware of this change…\u003cbr\u003e\nMaybe that string with the bad code has been there for decades and it was never run until this change was made…\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI don’t know if it’s worth talking about.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-09-22T21:14:54.841Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":79,"readers_count":78,"score":35.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":109116,"topic_slug":"pep-846-docstrings-for-type-ali